import Link from '../components/Link.astro';
import { routes } from '../data/site';
---

<TextPage
  title="Printing Guide: Paper, Size and Cutting for Party Printables"
  description="How to print party printables at home: paper weight, printing at 100% instead of Fit to page, cutting on the 0.25 pt dashed line, punching holes and hanging a banner."
  path="/printing-guide/"
  crumb="Printing guide"
  eyebrow="Before you print"
  heading="Printing guide."
  lede="Everything on this site is made for a home printer and ordinary paper. These are the four settings that decide whether the result looks like a shop-bought decoration."
  updated="2026-09-04"
>
  <h2>Print at 100%, never Fit to page</h2>
  <p>
    Every sheet is drawn at true size. <b>Fit to page</b> shrinks it by a few percent to clear the
    printer margin, and each flag comes out a slightly different width from the last batch. Choose
    <b>Actual size</b> in the print dialog, or set the scale to 100%.
  </p>
  <p>
    A4 and US Letter are separate files. Print the one that matches the paper in your tray: printing
    the A4 file on Letter reintroduces the same scaling problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Paper</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Piece</th><th>Paper</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Banner flags</td><td>90 to 120 gsm</td><td>Light enough to hang in a curve without pulling the string down</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Party hats</td><td>160 to 200 gsm matte card</td><td>Holds a cone shape and takes a glue tab</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cupcake toppers</td><td>160 to 200 gsm matte card</td><td>Stands up on a stick instead of flopping</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Signs</td><td>160 to 200 gsm matte card</td><td>Stands in a frame or small easel</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Favor tags</td><td>160 to 200 gsm matte card</td><td>Survives a punched hole and a knot</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Matte, not glossy. Glossy stock smudges under inkjet ink and reflects badly in photographs. If
    your printer struggles with card, feed it one sheet at a time through the rear or manual tray.
  </p>

  <h2>Ink</h2>
  <p>
    None of the sheets use a full-page colour fill: the background of every flag, sign and tag is
    the paper itself. That keeps a whole kit inside a fraction of a cartridge and stops thin paper
    from cockling. Print one test page before committing to a thirteen-letter banner, so you can
    check colour and scale on a single sheet.
  </p>

  <h2>Cutting</h2>
  <p>
    Cut along the dashed 0.25 pt outline. Scissors are fine for the straight edges; a craft knife
    and a metal ruler give a cleaner line on long cuts. Keep the small top margin on each banner
    flag, it holds the hanging holes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site credit sits outside the cut line on every sheet, so it ends up in the offcut and never
    on the finished decoration.
  </p>

  <h2>Assembly</h2>
  <p>
    <b>Banners.</b> Punch the two marked holes near the top corners and thread cotton string or
    narrow ribbon through the back of each flag. Leave a small gap between letters so the message
    curves naturally instead of hanging as a straight line.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Hats.</b> Cut the sector, roll it into a cone and glue the marked tab under the opposite
    edge. A small hole either side takes elastic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Toppers.</b> Cut two circles, tape a wooden pick between them and push into the frosting.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <b>Nothing prints?</b> Open the PDF in a real PDF reader rather than a browser preview: some
      browsers reflow the page and quietly rescale it. The paper size is stored in the file, so a
      reader that respects it will do the right thing at 100%.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Licence</h2>
  <p>
    Every file is free for personal use: your party, your classroom, your community event. Reselling
    the files or the printed pieces is not allowed. Full wording on the{' '}
    <Link route={routes.terms} class="text-link" label="terms of use" /> page.
  </p>
</TextPage>
